  "segments": [
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Chef Viktor is at the hospital after being hit by a car. The doctor examines his leg, but Viktor makes up a story to protect the person who hit him.",
      "gap": 0.8
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "У вас надрыв связок.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Это не очень страшно.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Не нагружайте ногу.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Спасибо.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Какая причина вашей травмы?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Я попал под машину.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Будете заявлять в полицию?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Нет.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Я забыл поставить машину на ручник.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "И она на меня наехала.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Понятно.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Заявлять не буду.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Now let's go back through the conversation line by line.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "У вас надрыв связок.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'You have a partial ligament tear.' The character combines 'at' with 'ligament' in a natural Russian turn of phrase.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Это не очень страшно.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'It's not too serious.' The character links 'this' directly with 'not' to express the thought clearly.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Не нагружайте ногу.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Keep your weight off that leg.' The character combines 'not' with 'foot' in a natural Russian turn of phrase.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Спасибо.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Thank you.' A crisp, everyday conversational reply meaning 'thank you' that you will hear constantly in Russian dialogue.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Какая причина вашей травмы?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'What is the cause of your injury?' There is no question word here — in Russian, rising intonation on 'injury' is enough to make it a question. Chef Viktor answers straight away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Я попал под машину.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I got hit by a car.' Notice how 'I' sets up the clause and leads naturally into 'car'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Будете заявлять в полицию?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Will you report to the police?' The character links 'will be' directly with 'declare' to express the thought clearly.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Нет.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'No.' A crisp, everyday conversational reply meaning 'no' that you will hear constantly in Russian dialogue.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Я забыл поставить машину на ручник.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I forgot to put the car on handbrake.' This everyday Russian sentence pairs 'I' with 'handbrake'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "И она на меня наехала.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'And it ran over me.' The '-a' ending on the past-tense verb for 'confront' shows that the subject is feminine.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Понятно.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I see.' A crisp, everyday conversational reply meaning 'clear' that you will hear constantly in Russian dialogue.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Заявлять не буду.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I will not report it.' The little negative word sits right in front of the verb for 'will be', just like 'not' in English. Now let's hear the whole thing once more.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
